@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: added `report --since DATE`. It accepts an ISO date or datetime. An invalid value gives a clean argparse error and exit code 2, with no traceback. An unreadable file or a malformed event line also gives a clean error and exit code 2.
+- `evlog/events.py`: added `as_utc` and `filter_since`. Event timestamps are compared in UTC. Naive timestamps count as UTC, as the README says legacy collectors write them. A bare date means midnight UTC.
+- `README.md`: documented `--since`.
+- `tests/test_since.py` (new): covers the UTC boundary, a datetime with an offset, no matches, a bad date and a missing file. No existing tests were touched.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest -v` ran 7 tests, including the teammate's two in `tests/test_report.py`, and all passed.
+- `python -m evlog report tests/__init__.py --since 2024-13-01` printed `invalid date ...` and exited with code 2.
+- `python -m evlog report /nonexist` printed `cannot read /nonexist: No such file or directory` and exited with code 2.
+- My first draft of the new test data was wrong because I mis-converted a timezone offset. I fixed the test data, and the code was not at fault.
+
+## Not verified / notes
+- "From that day onwards" is interpreted as midnight UTC, not in the ops team's local time zone. If ops expect local midnight, that needs a decision.
+- I only ran the commands above on one Python version, the default `python` in this environment. `datetime.fromisoformat` accepts fewer formats on Python older than 3.11, so for example a trailing `Z` may be rejected there.
+- I did not test the malformed-event-line error path with a dedicated test.
